A system to prevent abuse of server resources can be designed using a rate limiter that tracks the timing and frequency of incoming requests. This mechanism can be implemented using a queue combined with timestamps to monitor requests within a specified time frame, ensuring that they do not exceed a pre-defined limit. By utilizing a LinkedList to store request timestamps, the system can efficiently determine if a new request should be allowed or rejected based on the number of requests in the last second. If the number of requests surpasses the limit, the new requests will be rejected, thereby protecting server resources. Here's a practical implementation to illustrate the concept:

```java
import java.util.LinkedList;

public class RateLimiter {
    private final int maxRequestsPerSecond;
    private final LinkedList<Long> requestTimestamps;

    public RateLimiter(int maxRequestsPerSecond) {
        this.maxRequestsPerSecond = maxRequestsPerSecond;
        this.requestTimestamps = new LinkedList<>();
    }

    public synchronized boolean allowRequest() {
        long currentTimeMillis = System.currentTimeMillis();

        // Remove timestamps older than 1 second
        while (!requestTimestamps.isEmpty() && currentTimeMillis - requestTimestamps.peek() > 1000) {
            requestTimestamps.poll();
        }

        // Check if the number of requests in the last second exceeds the limit
        if (requestTimestamps.size() < maxRequestsPerSecond) {
            requestTimestamps.add(currentTimeMillis);
            return true; // Allow the request
        } else {
            return false; // Reject the request
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        RateLimiter rateLimiter = new RateLimiter(5); // Limit to 5 requests per second

        // Simulate requests
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            if (rateLimiter.allowRequest()) {
                System.out.println("Request " + (i + 1) + " allowed at " + System.currentTimeMillis());
            } else {
                System.out.println("Request " + (i + 1) + " denied due to rate limit");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

