To create a simple HTTP server in C++ that handles GET requests and returns a JSON response, we can use the Boost.Beast library, which provides a robust foundation for working with HTTP and WebSocket protocols over TCP/IP. The following code sets up a basic HTTP server that listens on a specified port and responds with a JSON object for any GET request. The server handles edge cases such as invalid requests and different HTTP methods by returning appropriate HTTP status codes. First, make sure you have Boost installed on your system. 

```cpp
#include <boost/beast/core.hpp>
#include <boost/beast/http.hpp>
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <boost/json.hpp>
#include <iostream>

namespace beast = boost::beast;     // from <boost/beast.hpp>
namespace http = beast::http;       // from <boost/beast/http.hpp>
namespace net = boost::asio;        // from <boost/asio.hpp>
using tcp = net::ip::tcp;           // from <boost/asio/ip/tcp.hpp>
