To protect a web service from potential abuse, we can implement user-specific request limitations using a combination of a HashMap and a token bucket algorithm. This involves storing user IDs and their respective request timestamps in a HashMap. The token bucket algorithm is utilized to track and limit the number of requests each user can make within a specific time frame, such as a maximum of 5 requests every 10 seconds. This setup supports multiple users and addresses edge cases such as bursts of requests immediately after a time window resets. Here's the code:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Map;

public class RateLimiter {

    private final int MAX_REQUESTS;
    private final long TIME_WINDOW_MS;
    private final Map<String, LinkedList<Long>> userRequestLog;

    public RateLimiter(int maxRequests, long timeWindowMs) {
        this.MAX_REQUESTS = maxRequests;
        this.TIME_WINDOW_MS = timeWindowMs;
        this.userRequestLog = new HashMap<>();
    }

    public boolean allowRequest(String userId) {
        long currentTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        userRequestLog.putIfAbsent(userId, new LinkedList<>());
        LinkedList<Long> requestTimes = userRequestLog.get(userId);

        // Remove timestamps that are outside the time window
        while (!requestTimes.isEmpty() && currentTime - requestTimes.peek() > TIME_WINDOW_MS) {
            requestTimes.poll();
        }

        if (requestTimes.size() < MAX_REQUESTS) {
            requestTimes.add(currentTime);
            return true;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RateLimiter rateLimiter = new RateLimiter(5, 10000);

        String userId = "user1";

        // Simulate 6 requests
        for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
            if (rateLimiter.allowRequest(userId)) {
                System.out.println("Request " + (i + 1) + " allowed.");
            } else {
                System.out.println("Request " + (i + 1) + " denied.");
    }

}
```

To implement a debouncing function in JavaScript, we create a higher-order function that returns a debounced version of the input function. The debounced function will delay the execution until after a specified wait time has elapsed since the last time it was invoked. This is useful for performance optimization in scenarios such as resizing the window or processing input events. Our approach leverages closures to retain the `timeoutId` across executions, ensuring that the function is only called once the wait time has passed without further invocations. The code follows best practices, including edge case handling, such as clearing timeouts to prevent memory leaks.

```javascript
// Debounce function definition
function debounce(func, wait) {
    // Timeout variable to store the ID of the timeout
    let timeoutId;

    // Return a debounced version of the original function
    return function(...args) {
        // Clear the previous timeout if it exists
        if (timeoutId) clearTimeout(timeoutId);

        // Set a new timeout for the provided wait time
        timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
            // Call the original function with the arguments
            func.apply(this, args);
        }, wait);
    };
}

// Example usage: Debounce a window resize event handler
function onResize() {
    console.log('Window resized!');
}

// Create a debounced version of the onResize function with a 300ms wait
const debouncedResize = debounce(onResize, 300);

// Add event listener for the resize event
window.addEventListener('resize', debouncedResize);
```

- **debounce Function**: This higher-order function takes another function `func` and a delay `wait` in milliseconds. It returns a new function that delays the execution of `func` until `wait` milliseconds have passed since the last invocation.
- **Timeout Management**: We use `clearTimeout` to prevent previously scheduled executions when the function is called multiple times in quick succession.

