One way to control how many requests are in flight at once is to introduce an explicit concurrency controller, often implemented as a semaphore. The semaphore tracks how many "slots" are currently in use and queues tasks when capacity is exhausted.

Here's a concrete TypeScript implementation that coordinates asynchronous API calls using a semaphore:

```typescript
class Semaphore {
    private tasks: (() => void)[] = [];
    private available: number;

    constructor(private capacity: number) {
        this.available = capacity;
    }

    public async acquire(): Promise<void> {
        if (this.available > 0) {
            this.available--;
            return Promise.resolve();
        }
        return new Promise(resolve => this.tasks.push(resolve));
    }

    public release(): void {
        this.available++;
        if (this.tasks.length > 0) {
            const next = this.tasks.shift();
            if (next) {
                this.available--;
                next();
            }
        }
    }
}

// Simulated API call
async function sendRequest(id: number): Promise<void> {
    console.log(`Sending request ${id}`);
    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => {
        console.log(`Completed request ${id}`);
        resolve();
    }, 1000)); // Simulating network delay
}

async function main() {
    const maxConcurrentRequests = 3; // N: max in-flight requests
    const semaphore = new Semaphore(maxConcurrentRequests);

    // Create 10 logical API tasks
    const requests = Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => async () => {
        // Wait until a concurrency slot is available
        await semaphore.acquire();
        try {
            await sendRequest(i);
        } finally {
            // Always release the slot, even on error
            semaphore.release();
        }
    });

    // Run all tasks; the semaphore enforces the concurrency cap
    await Promise.all(requests.map(request => request()));
}

main().catch(console.error);
```

How it coordinates asynchronous tasks:
- `Semaphore.capacity` defines the maximum number of concurrent operations.
- `available` tracks how many slots are currently free.
- `acquire()` either:
  - Immediately decrements `available` and returns if a slot is free, or
  - Returns a Promise that is stored in `tasks` and resolved later when a slot opens.
- `release()` increments `available` and, if there are queued tasks, takes the next waiting resolver, decrements `available` again (assigning the slot to that task), and calls it to unblock the waiting `acquire()`.

By wrapping each API call with `acquire()` and `release()` in a `try/finally`, you ensure that:
- No more than `maxConcurrentRequests` requests are in flight at once.
- Excess tasks are safely queued and eventually allowed to run.
- Slots are released even if an API call fails or throws, preventing deadlocks.

