To ensure fair resource allocation in a Java-based web application, implementing a rate limiter can be effective. This involves keeping track of the number of requests a user makes within a specified time window, such as 1 minute. A synchronized mechanism is essential to manage concurrent requests. Utilizing a `HashMap` to store each user's request timestamps and incorporating a cleanup system to discard outdated requests can help maintain balance. Here's a sample implementation:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class RateLimiter {
    private final int MAX_REQUESTS = 5;
    private final long TIME_WINDOW_MILLIS = 60 * 1000; // 1 minute
    private final Map<String, List<Long>> userRequestMap;

    public RateLimiter() {
        userRequestMap = new HashMap<>();
    }

    public synchronized boolean allowRequest(String userId) {
        long currentTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        userRequestMap.computeIfAbsent(userId, k -> new LinkedList<>());
        List<Long> timestamps = userRequestMap.get(userId);

        while (!timestamps.isEmpty() && timestamps.get(0) <= currentTime - TIME_WINDOW_MILLIS) {
            timestamps.remove(0);
        }

        if (timestamps.size() < MAX_REQUESTS) {
            timestamps.add(currentTime);
            return true;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        RateLimiter rateLimiter = new RateLimiter();
        String userId = "user1";

        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.println(rateLimiter.allowRequest(userId)); // should print true
        }

        System.out.println(rateLimiter.allowRequest(userId)); // should print false
    }
}

```

To manage temporary data storage efficiently in a high-traffic web application, we can implement an in-memory cache system with automatic expiration and cleanup. This involves using a HashMap combined with expiration times for each cache entry. We'll perform operations such as put, get, and delete, and ensure expired entries are automatically removed to optimize memory usage. Implementing a background thread for periodic cleanup ensures smooth operation without manual interference. Here's the implementation:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class InMemoryCache<K, V> {
    private class CacheItem {
        V value;
        long expiryTime;

        CacheItem(V value, long expiryTime) {
            this.value = value;
            this.expiryTime = expiryTime;
        }
    }

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, CacheItem> cacheMap;
    private final ScheduledExecutorService cleaner;

    public InMemoryCache() {
        cacheMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        cleaner = Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor();
        cleaner.scheduleAtFixedRate(this::cleanUp, 10, 10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    public void put(K key, V value, long ttlInMillis) {
        long expiryTime = System.currentTimeMillis() + ttlInMillis;
        cacheMap.put(key, new CacheItem(value, expiryTime));
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        CacheItem item = cacheMap.get(key);
        if (item == null || item.expiryTime < System.currentTimeMillis()) {
            cacheMap.remove(key);
            return null;
        }
        return item.value;
    }

    public void delete(K key) {
        cacheMap.remove(key);
    }

    private void cleanUp() {
        long currentTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        for (Map.Entry<K, CacheItem> entry : cacheMap.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue().expiryTime < currentTime) {
                cacheMap.remove(entry.getKey());
            }
        }
    }

    public void shutDown() {
        cleaner.shutdown();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        InMemoryCache<String, String> cache = new InMemoryCache<>();
        // Usage example
        cache.put("key1", "value1", 5000);
    }
}

