The Singleton pattern ensures that a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to it. A popular way to implement the Singleton pattern in Java with thread-safety and lazy initialization is through the use of the 'Double-Check Locking' mechanism. This approach minimizes the synchronization overhead by checking if the instance is already initialized before locking.

Here is how you can implement this:

```java
public class Singleton {
    // volatile ensures changes made by one thread are visible to other threads,
    // and prevents the compiler from reordering code.
    private static volatile Singleton instance;

    // Private constructor prevents instantiation from other classes
    private Singleton() {
    }

    // Method to return the singleton instance with lazy initialization and thread-safety
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) { // First check (no locking) - for performance
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) { // Second check (with locking) - for safety
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }

    // Example method to demonstrate usage
    public void showMessage() {
        System.out.println("Hello from Singleton!");
    }

    // Edge case: Make sure serialization maintains singleton property
    protected Object readResolve() {
        return getInstance();
    }
}

// Usage example:
public class SingletonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Singleton singleton = Singleton.getInstance();
        singleton.showMessage();
    }
}

**Explanation:**
- The `volatile` keyword is used to ensure that the instance variable is updated correctly across threads.
- The `getInstance` method checks if the instance is null before entering a synchronized block. This check avoids unnecessary synchronization after the instance is initialized.

