To efficiently handle a large number of API requests in TypeScript, preventing server overload while ensuring all requests are processed, you can implement a concurrency control mechanism like a 'semaphore'. This involves creating a queue of request tasks and controlling how many tasks run simultaneously. This can be achieved using Promises and a custom queue management system. Here's an implementation example:

```typescript
async function concurrentTaskRunner<T>(tasks: (() => Promise<T>)[], maxConcurrent: number): Promise<T[]> {
    const results: T[] = [];
    const taskQueue: (() => Promise<T>)[] = [...tasks]; // Clone task array to a queue
    const currentExecutions: Promise<void>[] = [];

    // Function to run a single task
    const runTask = async (task: () => Promise<T>, index: number) => {
        try {
            const result = await task();
            results[index] = result; // Store result by index
        } catch (error) {
            throw new Error(`Task at index ${index} failed: ${error.message}`);
        }
    };

    // Controlling the task execution based on the max concurrency allowed
    while (taskQueue.length > 0) {
        if (currentExecutions.length < maxConcurrent) {
            const taskIndex = tasks.length - taskQueue.length;
            const task = taskQueue.shift();
            if (task) {
                const taskPromise = runTask(task, taskIndex).finally(() => {
                    // Remove the completed task from current executions
                    const index = currentExecutions.indexOf(taskPromise);
                    if (index > -1) {
                        currentExecutions.splice(index, 1);
                    }
                });
                currentExecutions.push(taskPromise);
            }
        }
        // Wait for any of the tasks to complete if we reach max concurrency
        if (currentExecutions.length >= maxConcurrent) {
            await Promise.race(currentExecutions);
        }
    }
    await Promise.all(currentExecutions); // Ensure all remaining tasks complete
    return results;
}
```

Debouncing a function involves ensuring that it is not called too frequently. It is particularly useful for events that trigger many times in quick succession, such as window resize events. The approach involves using a timer to delay the execution of the function until after the event has stopped occurring for a specified time. This reduces the number of times the function is called, leading to better performance in scenarios like resizing the window. Here's how you can implement a debounce function in JavaScript:

```javascript
// Debounce function that delays the execution of a given function
function debounce(func, wait) {
    let timeout;
    return function executedFunction(...args) {
        const context = this;
        // Clear the timeout if it's already set
        clearTimeout(timeout);
        // Set a new timeout to call the function after the specified wait time
        timeout = setTimeout(() => {
            func.apply(context, args);
        }, wait);
    };
}

// Example usage: Debouncing a window resize event
const handleResize = debounce(() => {
    console.log('Window resized to:', window.innerWidth, 'x', window.innerHeight);
}, 200);

// Add the debounced function as an event listener
window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);
```

### Explanation:
- `debounce`: This function takes another function (`func`) and a delay time in milliseconds (`wait`) as arguments. It returns a new function that, when executed, clears any existing timeout and sets a new one.
- `clearTimeout(timeout)`: Ensures that the previously set timeout is cleared, preventing unnecessary function calls if the event keeps triggering quickly.
- `setTimeout`: After the `wait` time elapses without further calls, the function `func` is executed.
- `handleResize`: Demonstrates the debounce function to handle window resize events efficiently. The function will only log the window's dimensions 200ms after the resizing stops, improving performance during rapid resize events.

In this solution, we will create a simple REST API server using the C++ library "cpp-httplib" to handle GET requests. The server will respond with a JSON object when accessing the '/api/data' endpoint. We will also include error handling for unsupported HTTP methods (such as POST) and invalid routes. The cpp-httplib library is chosen for its simplicity and ease of use in setting up HTTP servers in C++. Below is the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <httplib.h>
#include <json/json.h>

int main() {
    httplib::Server svr;

    // Endpoint to handle GET requests
    svr.Get("/api/data", [](const httplib::Request&, httplib::Response& res) {
        // Create a JSON object
        Json::Value jsonData;
        jsonData["key"] = "value";
        jsonData["number"] = 123;

        // Convert JSON object to a string
        Json::StreamWriterBuilder writer;
        std::string jsonString = Json::writeString(writer, jsonData);

        // Set response headers and body
        res.set_header("Content-Type", "application/json");
        res.set_content(jsonString, "application/json");
    });

    // Handler for unsupported methods
    svr.Post("/api/data", [](const httplib::Request&, httplib::Response& res) {
        res.status = 405; // Method Not Allowed
        res.set_content("Method Not Allowed", "text/plain");
    });

    // Handle invalid routes
    svr.set_error_handler([](const auto&, auto& res) {
        res.status = 404; // Not Found
        res.set_content("404 Not Found", "text/plain");
    });

    std::cout << "Server started at http://localhost:8080\n";
    svr.listen("localhost", 8080);

    return 0;
}
```

