In concurrent programming, managing the number of simultaneous operations is crucial, especially when dealing with API limits or resource constraints. We can implement a simple concurrency control mechanism in TypeScript using promises to ensure that only a specific number of API calls are made at the same time. This is often referred to as a 'concurrency pool'.

Here's an approach using Promises and async/await to limit the number of concurrent API requests:

```typescript
const apiCall = async (url: string): Promise<any> => {
    // Simulate an API call with a timeout
    return new Promise((resolve) => {
        setTimeout(() => {
            resolve(`Fetched data from ${url}`);
        }, 1000);
    });
};

async function concurrentApiCalls(urls: string[], maxConcurrent: number): Promise<any[]> {
    const results: any[] = [];
    const executing: Promise<void>[] = [];

    for (const url of urls) {
        const promise = apiCall(url).then(result => results.push(result));
        executing.push(promise);

        if (executing.length >= maxConcurrent) {
            // Wait for the earliest promise to resolve before adding a new one
            await Promise.race(executing);
            // Remove resolved promises to maintain the concurrency limit
            executing.splice(executing.findIndex(p => p === promise), 1);
        }
    }

    // Wait for all remaining promises to resolve
    await Promise.all(executing);
    return results;
}

(async () => {
    const urls = [
        'https://api.example.com/resource1',
        'https://api.example.com/resource2',
        'https://api.example.com/resource3',
        'https://api.example.com/resource4',
        'https://api.example.com/resource5'
    ];
    const maxConcurrentRequests = 2;
    const results = await concurrentApiCalls(urls, maxConcurrentRequests);
    console.log(results);
})();
```

### Approach Explanation:
1. **Simulating API Calls**: We created a function `apiCall` that simulates a network request with a delay using a timeout.

