You can prototype a lightweight HTTP microservice in C++ using Boost.Asio for networking and Boost.Beast for HTTP parsing/serialization. The core pieces are:

1. An `io_context` to drive asynchronous operations.
2. A `tcp::acceptor` to listen for incoming connections.
3. Per-connection logic to:
   - Read an HTTP request
   - Check the method and target
   - Build and send an HTTP response

Below is a compact example illustrating a minimal asynchronous HTTP endpoint that:
- Listens on a given port
- Accepts connections
- Handles only GET requests
- Responds with:
  - `200 OK` and a "Hello, world!" body for `/`
  - `404 Not Found` for other paths
  - `405 Method Not Allowed` for non-GET methods

```cpp
#include <boost/beast/core.hpp>
#include <boost/beast/http.hpp>
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <iostream>

using tcp = boost::asio::ip::tcp;
namespace http = boost::beast::http;

// Build an HTTP response based on the request
void handle_request(const http::request<http::string_body>& req,
                    http::response<http::string_body>& res)
{
    res.version(req.version());
    res.set(http::field::server, "Boost.Beast HTTP Microservice");

    if (req.method() != http::verb::get) {
        res.result(http::status::method_not_allowed);
        res.body() = "Method not allowed";
        res.prepare_payload();
        return;
    }

    if (req.target() == "/") {
        res.result(http::status::ok);
        res.body() = "Hello, world!";
    } else {
        res.result(http::status::not_found);
        res.body() = "Resource not found";
    }

    res.prepare_payload();
}

// Session: represents a single connection
class session : public std::enable_shared_from_this<session> {
    tcp::socket socket_;
    boost::beast::flat_buffer buffer_;
    http::request<http::string_body> req_;
    http::response<http::string_body> res_;

public:
    explicit session(tcp::socket socket)
        : socket_(std::move(socket)) {}

    void run() { read_request(); }

private:
    void read_request() {
        auto self = shared_from_this();
        http::async_read(socket_, buffer_, req_,
            [self](boost::beast::error_code ec, std::size_t) {
                if (!ec) {
                    handle_request(self->req_, self->res_);
                    self->write_response();
                }
            });
    }

    void write_response() {
        auto self = shared_from_this();
        http::async_write(socket_, res_,
            [self](boost::beast::error_code ec, std::size_t) {
                // Gracefully close the socket after sending the response
                self->socket_.shutdown(tcp::socket::shutdown_send, ec);
            });
    }
};

// Accepts incoming connections and starts sessions
class server {
    boost::asio::io_context& ioc_;
    tcp::acceptor acceptor_;

public:
    server(boost::asio::io_context& ioc, tcp::endpoint endpoint)
        : ioc_(ioc), acceptor_(ioc)
    {
        boost::beast::error_code ec;
        acceptor_.open(endpoint.protocol(), ec);
        acceptor_.set_option(boost::asio::socket_base::reuse_address(true), ec);
        acceptor_.bind(endpoint, ec);
        acceptor_.listen(boost::asio::socket_base::max_listen_connections, ec);
        do_accept();
    }

private:
    void do_accept() {
        acceptor_.async_accept(
            [this](boost::beast::error_code ec, tcp::socket socket) {
                if (!ec) {
                    std::make_shared<session>(std::move(socket))->run();
                }
                do_accept(); // accept the next connection
            });
    }
};

int main() {
    try {
        boost::asio::io_context ioc;
        tcp::endpoint endpoint(tcp::v4(), 8080); // listen on port 8080
        server srv(ioc, endpoint);
        ioc.run();
    } catch (std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << "Error: " << e.what() << '\n';
    }
}
```

Key components:
- **`io_context`**: Acts as the event loop that dispatches asynchronous operations.
- **`tcp::acceptor`**: Listens on a port and asynchronously accepts new TCP connections.
- **`session`**: Manages a single client connection, reading an HTTP request and writing a response.
- **`handle_request`**: Encapsulates your microservice logic: method validation, path routing, and status codes.

This pattern scales to more endpoints and more complex logic by extending `handle_request` (or replacing it with a router) while keeping the asynchronous I/O and connection handling structure the same.

In Go, you can build an extensible geometry library by modeling polymorphism with interfaces and concrete types rather than classical inheritance. A common pattern is to define a `Shape` interface and then implement it for various structs, such as `Circle` and `Rectangle`. You can also centralize input validation (e.g., non-positive dimensions) inside each type’s methods so callers don’t need to repeat checks.

Here’s an example of such a library:

```go
package geometry

import "math"

// Shape represents any geometric object that can report its area.
// This interface is the core abstraction that enables extensibility:
// new shapes can be added simply by implementing Area().
type Shape interface {
    Area() float64
}

// Circle represents a circle with a given radius.
type Circle struct {
    Radius float64
}

// Area computes the area of the circle.
// If Radius is non-positive, it returns 0 to handle invalid cases safely.
func (c Circle) Area() float64 {
    if c.Radius <= 0 {
        return 0
    }
    return math.Pi * c.Radius * c.Radius
}

// Rectangle represents a rectangle with width and height.
type Rectangle struct {
    Width, Height float64
}

// Area computes the area of the rectangle.
// If either dimension is non-positive, it returns 0 to avoid invalid areas.
func (r Rectangle) Area() float64 {
    if r.Width <= 0 || r.Height <= 0 {
        return 0
    }
    return r.Width * r.Height
}
```

Client code (e.g., in a `main` package) can then use this library in an object-oriented style by working only with the `Shape` interface:

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "yourmodule/geometry" // replace with the actual module path
)

func main() {
    // Valid shapes
    c := geometry.Circle{Radius: 3}
    r := geometry.Rectangle{Width: 5, Height: 10}

    // Shapes with invalid dimensions
    cEdge := geometry.Circle{Radius: -1}
    rEdge := geometry.Rectangle{Width: 0, Height: 10}

    // A slice of the Shape interface allows mixing different concrete types.
