To design a lazily initialized, globally accessible configuration object that is safe under high concurrency and efficient, you can use a thread-safe Singleton implementation with Double-Check Locking and a volatile instance field.

Example implementation:

```java
public class AppConfig {
    // volatile ensures safe publication across threads
    private static volatile AppConfig instance;

    // private constructor prevents external instantiation
    private AppConfig() {
        // potentially expensive initialization logic
        // e.g., loading settings from files or remote services
    }

    public static AppConfig getInstance() {
        // First check without locking for performance
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (AppConfig.class) {
                // Second check with locking to ensure only one instance is created
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new AppConfig();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }

    // Example method to demonstrate usage
    public String getSetting(String key) {
        // return configuration value for key
        return "someValue";
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AppConfig config1 = AppConfig.getInstance();
        AppConfig config2 = AppConfig.getInstance();

        System.out.println("Same config instance? " + (config1 == config2)); // true
        System.out.println(config1.getSetting("db.url"));
    }
}
```

Why this avoids race conditions:
- The `synchronized` block ensures that only one thread at a time can execute the critical section where the instance is created.
- The second `if (instance == null)` inside the synchronized block guarantees that even if two threads pass the first check concurrently, only one will actually construct the object.
- The `volatile` keyword prevents instruction reordering and guarantees visibility of the fully constructed `AppConfig` instance to all threads. Without `volatile`, another thread could see a reference to a partially constructed object.

Why this avoids performance bottlenecks:
- The method does not synchronize on every call. Once `instance` is initialized, most calls to `getInstance()` only perform the fast, non-synchronized null check and return the already created object.
- Synchronization occurs only during the first few calls while the instance is being created, which is typically negligible compared to the lifetime of the application.

This design gives you:
- Lazy initialization (object is created only when first needed).
- Thread safety under high concurrency.
- Minimal synchronization overhead after initialization, making it suitable for performance-sensitive systems that frequently access a global configuration.

