This design gives you a simple, in-memory, uniqueness-enforcing catalog suitable for a small digital bookstore or as the core of a more complex system.

In TypeScript, you can manage multiple asynchronous tasks and control concurrent execution using a semaphore mechanism by combining promises with a counter to limit the number of concurrently running tasks. This is useful when dealing with resource-intensive operations, ensuring your system doesn't get overwhelmed. Below is an example with explanation:

```typescript
// Define a semaphore class to manage concurrent execution
class Semaphore {
    private tasks: (() => Promise<void>)[] = [];
    private availableSlots: number;

    constructor(maxConcurrent: number) {
        this.availableSlots = maxConcurrent;
    }

    // Function to acquire a slot
    public async acquire(): Promise<void> {
        if (this.availableSlots > 0) {
            this.availableSlots--;
            return Promise.resolve();
        } else {
            return new Promise(resolve => this.tasks.push(resolve));
        }
    }

    // Function to release a slot
    public release(): void {
        this.availableSlots++;
        if (this.tasks.length > 0) {
            const nextTask = this.tasks.shift();
            if (nextTask) nextTask();
            this.availableSlots--;
        }
    }
}

