In C++, you can leverage functional programming techniques using the Standard Template Library (STL) to transform and filter collections. Here, we will demonstrate how to square each integer in a list and then filter out the even numbers using STL algorithms like `std::transform` and `std::remove_if` along with lambda expressions. This approach makes code more concise and adheres to the functional programming paradigm by treating functions as first-class citizens.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    // Initial list of integers
    std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    // Transform each number by squaring it
    std::transform(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), numbers.begin(), [](int x) {
        return x * x;
    });

    // Remove even numbers from the list
