In functional programming, a pure function is one that, given the same input, will always return the same output and does not cause any side effects. Let's implement a pure function in C++ to calculate the factorial of a number. This function will not modify any external state or perform I/O operations. 

### Approach
The function `factorial` will recursively calculate the factorial of a given positive integer. For edge cases, we will handle inputs such as negative numbers by returning an indication of error, since a factorial is only defined for non-negative integers.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <optional>

// Define a pure function to calculate factorial
std::optional<unsigned long long> factorial(int n) {
    // Handle edge case: negative input
    if (n < 0) {
        return std::nullopt; // Indicate an error for negative input
    }
    // Base case
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    // Recursive case
    return n * factorial(n - 1).value();
}

int main() {
    int number = 5; // Example input
    auto result = factorial(number);

    if (result) {
        std::cout << "Factorial of " << number << " is " << result.value() << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Error: Factorial is not defined for negative numbers." << std::endl;
    }

    // Test edge case
    int negativeNumber = -5;
    auto negativeResult = factorial(negativeNumber);

    if (negativeResult) {
        std::cout << "Factorial of " << negativeNumber << " is " << negativeResult.value() << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Error: Factorial is not defined for negative numbers." << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

You can build a reusable debouncing utility that wraps any handler so it only runs after the user stops clicking for a specified delay. This prevents multiple rapid calls (including duplicate API requests) from being fired.

```javascript
// Generic debounce utility
