To optimize performance in real-time applications, JavaScript developers can utilize a debouncing technique. This involves using closures to handle timeouts, ensuring that a specific function is executed only after a designated delay if it hasn't been called again. This approach helps minimize unnecessary computational overhead, especially during frequent events like input changes, by limiting the number of function executions until a user has ceased interacting for a set time. Here's a sample implementation:

```javascript
// Debounce function implementation
function debounce(fn, delay) {
    let timeoutId;
    return function(...args) {
        // Clear the previous timeout if it exists
        if (timeoutId) {
            clearTimeout(timeoutId);
        }
        // Set a new timeout
        timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
            fn(...args);
        }, delay);
    };
}

// Example usage: debouncing an input event listener
