You can build a small concurrency controller that takes an array of task factories (functions that return Promises) and a concurrency limit, then runs them while ensuring no more than the allowed number are in flight. It also collects all results, including failures.

Here’s one way to implement such a utility:

```typescript
interface TaskResult {
  data: string; // or a more general type
  error?: Error;
}

async function runWithConcurrencyLimit(
  taskFactories: Array<() => Promise<TaskResult>>,
  limit: number
): Promise<TaskResult[]> {
  const results: TaskResult[] = [];
  const executing: Promise<void>[] = [];

  const runTask = async (taskFactory: () => Promise<TaskResult>) => {
    try {
      const result = await taskFactory();
      results.push(result);
    } catch (err) {
      results.push({ data: "", error: err as Error });
    }
  };

  for (const taskFactory of taskFactories) {
    const p = runTask(taskFactory).finally(() => {
      const index = executing.indexOf(p);
      if (index >= 0) executing.splice(index, 1);
    });

    executing.push(p);

    // If we hit the concurrency limit, wait until at least one finishes
    if (executing.length >= limit) {
      await Promise.race(executing);
    }
  }

  // Wait for any remaining tasks to finish
  await Promise.all(executing);

  return results;
}

// Example: wrapping API calls as task factories
const apiCall1 = () =>
  new Promise<TaskResult>((resolve) =>
    setTimeout(() => resolve({ data: "Result 1" }), 1000)
  );

const apiCall2 = () =>
  new Promise<TaskResult>((resolve) =>
    setTimeout(() => resolve({ data: "Result 2" }), 2000)
  );

const apiCall3 = () =>
  new Promise<TaskResult>((resolve, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("Request failed")), 1500)
  );

const apiCall4 = () =>
  new Promise<TaskResult>((resolve) =>
    setTimeout(() => resolve({ data: "Result 4" }), 500)
  );

async function main() {
  const tasks = [apiCall1, apiCall2, apiCall3, apiCall4];

  const results = await runWithConcurrencyLimit(tasks, 3);

  for (const r of results) {
    if (r.error) {
      console.error("Task failed:", r.error.message);
    } else {
      console.log("Task succeeded with data:", r.data);
    }
  }
}

main().catch(console.error);
```

This pattern:
- Accepts tasks as functions returning Promises, so they are only started when the controller decides.
- Uses an `executing` array to track in-flight tasks.
- Uses `Promise.race` to wait until at least one running task finishes before starting another when the limit is reached.
- Catches individual task errors and stores them alongside successful results, instead of rejecting the entire batch.

You can reuse `runWithConcurrencyLimit` anywhere you need controlled parallelism for asynchronous operations, such as API calls, file processing, or database queries.

